Subject: [PATCH] const-correct MIB tables, drop 71 dead locals, ANSI ttcp.c

Two independent cleanups bundled because they touch overlapping files.

* src/maint/hediag.c: struct reg_table's `name` is a read-only label - it is
  only compared against NULL and returned by reg_name(), which itself is used
  solely in a NULL test and a printf("%s"). Making the member and the return
  type const char * removes all 83 -Wdiscarded-qualifiers warnings in this file
  with no cast and no behaviour change.

* AsnOcts/AsnOid MIB definition tables (rfc1155_smi.c, sysgroup.c, atmf_uni.c,
  message.c): 45 more -Wdiscarded-qualifiers, and here a const member is not
  available. AsnOcts.octs is used both ways in this codebase - the static
  definition tables initialise it from string literals, while the decode paths
  treat the same field as a writable heap buffer:
      ilmid/io.c:94          name->octs[n++] = '\201';
      asn1/asn_octs.c:247    result->octs[len] = '\0';
      ilmid/message.c:181    entry->name.octs[ADDRESS_LEN] = '\24';
      memcpy destinations    io.c:81, message.c:69/107/148/180, atmf_uni.c:240,
                             mib.c:372, ilmid.c:304/313, wait.c:71
      free()                 atmf_uni.c:135/142, wait.c:40-54, actions.c:35
      realloc via alloc()    io.c:80/88, message.c:67/105/146/178, atmf_uni.c:230/239
  Turning the member into const unsigned char */const char * would break all of
  those, and splitting AsnOcts into a read-only view plus a mutable buffer would
  rewrite the whole 1992-era asn1 library and every consumer of it for a code
  path (ILMI/SNMP) that this tree does not exercise. So each literal that gcc
  names gets an explicit (char *), which is the documented idiom in C for
  -Wwrite-strings and is provably safe per object: the objects touched here are
  static tables whose bytes are never written through. The alternative - dropping
  the -Wwrite-strings that configure.ac line 57 pushes into CFLAGS - would clear
  all 128 at once but changes the warning posture of every file, so it is left
  for an explicit decision.

* 71 dead local declarations removed. In rfc1155_smi.c and rfc1157_snmp.c these
  are snacc 1.7 boilerplate: the generator emits seqDone, totalElmtsLen1/2,
  elmtLen1/2, tagId1/2, mandatoryElmtCount1, listLen and component for every
  SEQUENCE or CHOICE member regardless of whether it is optional, so the same
  name is live in one function and dead in the next. Removals are therefore
  driven strictly by the (file, line) the compiler reported, never by name, and
  each candidate was re-checked to be a single-declarator line whose initializer
  (if any) is a constant. snacc is not part of this build - the generated .c
  files ship in the tarball - so the edits are permanent. Also removed: ttcp.c's
  `static char RCSid[]`, referenced nowhere in the file.

* src/test/ttcp.c also has its nine remaining K&R definitions (pattern, outfmt,
  getrusage, read_timer, prusage, psecs, Nread, Nwrite, mread) converted to ANSI
  prototypes, taking -Wold-style-definition with it. Parameter types are copied
  verbatim from each old-style declaration run; prusage's single multi-declarator
  line `register struct rusage *r0, *r1;` becomes two parameters.

* src/mpoad/p_factory.c send_cache_imposition_reply(): `common` is assigned but
  never read, so the declaration goes - but not the assignment's right-hand
  side, which is a memcpy() that actually builds the reply's NHRP common header.
  The memcpy is kept as a bare call.

* src/ilmid/atmf_uni.c:246 and one atmf_uni.c local are deliberately NOT
  touched: `NetPrefixNode *tmp = (NetPrefixNode *) var->value;` is the lvalue
  that Q_REMOVE assigns its head-update result to, so it cannot be deleted
  without changing the expansion. That the result is discarded is a separate
  latent question about whether var->value should be written back, which is not
  something to change blind in an unexercised path.
